    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d113">

                <pb facs="0296" n="168" sameAs="frus:frus1885/pg168"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 113.</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Young to Mr.
                            Bayard</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-03-30"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 30, 1885</date>.
                        (Received May 13.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 696.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inclose a
                    correspondence between the legation and the consul at Ningpo.</p>
                <p>I have thought it well to say to the consul that as China and France are at peace
                    with the United States, as we are officially informed that <pb facs="0297"
                        n="169" xml:id="pg169"/> a state of war exists between the two nations, and
                    as it is our duty to maintain an exact neutrality, he would be justified in
                    refusing to enter or clear any vessels under the American flag supplying either
                    belligerent with contraband of war.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JOHN RUSSELL YOUNG.</signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d113-a1.xml" xpointer="d113-a1"/>
